## 2.5 What Turiya is not

Mantra 7 defines Turiya mostly by **negation**:

| Negation | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Not inward-conscious | Not the dreamer |
| Not outward-conscious | Not the waker |
| Not both | Not the in-between drowsy state |
| Not a mass of consciousness | Not the deep sleeper (and not an all-knowing God) |
| Not conscious, not unconscious | Not "knowing objects," but not inert like a rock |
| Unseen, beyond transaction, ungraspable | Not an object of any sense or action |
| Without marks, unthinkable, unnameable | Cannot be inferred, conceived, or named |

### Turiya is not a fourth *state*
- It's not a special experience you reach in meditation, sitting alongside the other three.
- **Example:** looking for Turiya apart from the three states is like **throwing away the bangle, necklace and ring to search for the gold**. You will find nothing. The gold was in them all along.

### Why it can't be named (Shankara's analysis)
Words pick things out in five ways. Turiya fits none:

| Way of naming | Example | Why it fails for Turiya |
|---|---|---|
| Class | "That's a cow" | No class of Turiyas |
| Quality | "The red flower" | It has no qualities |
| Action | "Call the cook" | It does nothing |
| Relation | "The teacher" (of students) | Nothing exists apart from it to relate to |
| Convention | "His name is Prakash" | It can't be pointed to as one thing among others |

